// expect-error: R006
//
// R006 — `#[trusted]` requires `#[ring(outer)]`.
//
// Before step 21 of the supremum loop (axis 6, "fewer escape hatches")
// this exact source compiled cleanly and produced WASM with a
// `handle Unsafe` block running in the inner ring. That bypassed the
// ring discipline: inner-ring code is the safe-by-construction policy
// tier and must never claim trust.
//
// ABUSE VECTOR (verified against the pre-change commit):
// `#[ring(inner)] #[trusted]` was accepted by the parser AND by the
// effect-check pass (which checked `module.trusted` without consulting
// `module.ring`). The combination granted `handle Unsafe` access in the
// inner ring — exactly the privilege the ring system is meant to
// quarantine to the outer ring.
//
// After step 21, the type-check pass emits R006 for any module that
// declares `#[trusted]` without `#[ring(outer)]`. The diagnostic names
// the module by name and offers two copyable fixes (add the ring or
// drop the trust).
#[ring(inner)] #[trusted]
module ext;

fn do_thing() ! { Unsafe } {
    handle Unsafe {
        let _x: i64 = 1;
    };
    return;
}
